    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d669">

                <pb facs="0706" n="608" xml:id="pg608"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.5034 Business Tax/62</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China (Johnson) to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 1888</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1932-12-24">December 24, 1932</date>.<lb/>[Received January 30,
                        1933.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: With further reference to the Legation’s
                    despatch No. 1871, December 21, 1932, regarding the attempts of the Chinese
                    authorities to enforce the business tax upon American firms, I have the honor to
                    enclose copies of the memorandum of a conversation I had on December 17th with
                    M. Wilden, the French Minister, and Mr. Ingram, the British Chargé
                        d’Affaires.<anchor n="64" xml:id="d669-f1"/></p>
                <p>I informed my colleagues that, while we were protesting against the collection of
                    this tax upon American firms, we were not endeavoring to prevent American
                    merchants from dealing with the matter in any practical manner they might deem
                    advisable.</p>
                <p>Respectfully yours,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Nelson Trusler
                    Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
